1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:06,961
In the late 1980s, in our small town,
radio was the window to the world. News,

2
00:00:06,961 --> 00:00:11,401
music, football – everything came
through the radio.

3
00:00:12,050 --> 00:00:16,934
When my father was still young, he found
a broken radio. Instead of throwing it

4
00:00:16,934 --> 00:00:19,531
away, he opened it to see what was
inside.

5
00:00:20,175 --> 00:00:26,010
He had no teacher or books. By the light
of a kerosene lamp, night after night,

6
00:00:26,010 --> 00:00:28,226
he carefully traced the wires.

7
00:00:28,883 --> 00:00:33,224
He worked tirelessly until the radio
spoke again.

8
00:00:36,883 --> 00:00:43,703
Word spread. Neighbors started bringing
their radios. His table filled with

9
00:00:43,703 --> 00:00:45,704
wires and spare parts.

10
00:00:46,342 --> 00:00:51,160
On Saturdays, people waited outside his
door so they wouldn't miss the news or

11
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:52,333
the football match.

12
00:00:53,342 --> 00:00:59,062
He once told me: "Everything that is
broken can be understood. You just have

13
00:00:59,062 --> 00:01:00,643
to open it and look."

14
00:01:01,342 --> 00:01:07,311
Today, I fix things too. Not radios, but
code. When a program breaks, I open it

15
00:01:07,311 --> 00:01:09,653
and look, just as he taught me.

16
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:17,335
His table became my laptop. The same
curiosity, a new kind of wire. The Radio

17
00:01:17,335 --> 00:01:18,431
Man's child.
